<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.15.2: Determining if Points on Boundary Lines Are Solutions to a System</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00649</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.15.2: Determining if Points on Boundary Lines Are Solutions to a System</md:title>
    <md:uuid>88209c5c-94bd-4530-9150-a899a48ce139</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity</title>
<para>Here are the graphs of the inequalities in this system:</para>
<!--BEGIN SYSTEM OF EQUATIONS -->
<para>\(\left\{\begin{array}{l}x\;\lt\;y\\y\geq\;-2x\;-\;6\end{array}\right.\)</para>
<!--END SYSTEM OF EQUATIONS --><!--BEGIN SYSTEM OF EQUATIONS-->
<div>
  <div><figure><media alt="A graph of two intersecting inequalities on a coordinate plane."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/d4029ba11276782f6550954c13749b37655b6b85"/></media></figure></div>
</div>
<para>Decide whether each point is a solution to the system. Be prepared to explain how you know.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((3,-5)\)</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <div class="body">Enter your answer here:</div>
  <div class="body"/>
    <div class="body">Compare your answer:</div>
    <div class="body">
      <para>No. \((3,-5)\) is in the shaded region containing the solutions to one of the inequalities but not in the region where the shading overlaps.</para></div>
  </solution></exercise>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((0,5)\)</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <div class="body">Enter your answer here:</div>
  <div class="body"/>
    <div class="body">Compare your answer:</div>
    <div class="body">
      <para>Yes. \((0,5)\) is in the region where the solutions to the two inequalities overlap.</para></div>
  </solution></exercise>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((-6,6)\)</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <div class="body">Enter your answer here:</div>
  <div class="body"/>
    <div class="body">Compare your answer:</div>
    <div class="body">
      <para>Yes. (\(-6,6)\) is on the solid boundary of \(y\geq-2x-6\) because \(0=-2(-3)−6\), and it is within the shaded region representing solutions to \(x&lt;y\).
      </para></div>
  </solution></exercise>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((3,3)\)</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <div class="body">Enter your answer here:</div>
  <div class="body"/>
    <div class="body">Compare your answer:</div>
    <div class="body">
      <para>No. While \((3,3)\) is clearly a solution to \(y\geq-2x-6\), it is on the dashed boundary of \(x &lt; y\). The statement \(3&lt;3\) is not true.
      </para></div>
  </solution></exercise>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>\((-2,-2)\)</item>
</list>
<exercise>
  <problem>
  </problem><solution>
    
    <div class="body">Enter your answer here:</div>
  <div class="body"/>
    <div class="body">Compare your answer:</div>
    <div class="body">
      <para>No, because \(-2&lt;-2\) is not true. Even though it is on the solid boundary of one, it is on the dashed boundary of the other.
      </para></div>
  </solution></exercise>
<title> Video:</title>
<para>Watch the following video to learn more </para>
<div class="os-raise-d-flex-nowrap os-raise-justify-content-center">
